{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.65
    ,
      "why": "Primary area-minimiser. Both pipelined families give 1 result/cycle, so the >=1 MSPS constraint is met by any Fmax above ~1 MHz and is not binding; the real constraint is max_abs_err <= 2^-10 and the objective is cell area. Registering only every m-th micro-rotation (m up to 8) removes most of the per-stage pipeline FFs, which is the dominant area term for this family, at the cost of a longer combinational path and lower Fmax - which the loose throughput constraint tolerates. This family therefore owns the low-area end of the Pareto front. Ranges are centred on the region where accuracy can plausibly reach 2^-10 (W around 13-18, N around 12-18) while still allowing code to find the exact feasibility boundary on the low side."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.35
    ,
      "why": "Reference / high-Fmax anchor for the front. One register per micro-rotation maximises Fmax but also FF count, so it should be dominated on area by pipelined_m; it is kept to (a) confirm the accuracy-vs-area boundary is family-independent (accuracy depends only on W, N, guards, rounding), (b) provide the high-throughput end of the trade-off curve that hypervolume rewards, and (c) give a clean baseline against which pipelined_m's area savings are measured. Same accuracy-oriented parameter ranges as pipelined_m so the two fronts are directly comparable."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Throughput is not the binding constraint: both allowed families emit 1 result/cycle, so >=1 MSPS only requires Fmax >= ~1 MHz, which even heavily register-collapsed designs clear. The binding constraint is max_abs_err <= 2^-10, and the objective is minimum cell area with accuracy_bits as the second objective. Accuracy is a function of data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding only, so the search should concentrate on the smallest (W, N, guard) combination that still clears 2^-10, and on the register structure that minimises FFs for that datapath. pipelined_m is the natural area winner (fewer pipeline registers via m) and gets the larger share; pipelined is retained as the high-Fmax anchor and cross-check. Ranges are deliberately centred on the plausible feasible accuracy region (W ~ 13-18, N ~ 12-18) rather than the full 8..28 / 4..30 registry, so the 100-eval budget is spent near the feasibility boundary and along the area/accuracy trade-off, with code free to widen later once the front is located."
}
